faq:
  - q: "What is different about a second interview?"
    a: "First interviews screen for basic fit and calibrate level. Second interviews go deeper: more senior evaluators, more specific behavioral and strategic questions, sometimes a case study or presentation component, and direct assessment of whether you can operate at the level required. You should expect harder questions about specific decisions you made, more pushback on your answers, and more stakeholder-facing scenarios."
  - q: "How do I prepare for a second interview?"
    a: "Five steps: (1) Review your first-interview performance — what questions did you handle weakly? (2) Assume the second-round evaluators have read your resume — prepare for nuanced follow-up on specific roles and decisions. (3) Prepare a deeper set of STAR stories for leadership, failure, and high-stakes execution — second rounds typically go harder on these. (4) Research the senior evaluators specifically on LinkedIn. (5) Prepare a specific signal of conviction: 'After the first conversation, I am even more interested in this role because [specific reason].'"
  - q: "Should I ask for feedback between interview rounds?"
    a: "Yes — if you have a recruiter contact. Ask: 'Is there anything from the first conversation I should be prepared to go deeper on in round 2?' Recruiters sometimes share focus areas. Even vague guidance ('they will probe more on your leadership experience') is useful. The worst outcome is they say they cannot share — and even asking signals self-awareness and preparation."
  - q: "How do I close a second interview?"
    a: "Express specific conviction, not generic enthusiasm. 'Based on our conversations, I am genuinely excited about this role — specifically [one concrete reason]. I want to be clear that this is a top-priority opportunity for me. Is there anything from my background you want me to address before you move to the next step?' This opens the door for objection-handling and signals commitment without being desperate."
  - q: "What questions come up more in second interviews than first interviews?"
    a: "Second rounds typically probe: (1) Specific decisions — not just what you did, but why you chose that approach over alternatives. (2) Failure and recovery — how you handled things when they went wrong. (3) Leadership and influence — how you moved outcomes with people who did not report to you. (4) Long-term goals — where you are heading and how this role fits. (5) Offer-stage signals — some second-round interviewers will probe your other opportunities, timeline, and compensation expectations."
